WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Can you save Mario's girl from the clutches of Donkey Kong?

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:26.540
And welcome to NEStalgia, the only podcast exploring every NES game in chronological order.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.800
I'm Michael Esposito.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:28.960
I'm Sean Tresmond.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.380
And that's it today.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.620
It's a very intimate conversation between me and the Sean.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.640
That's me. Yeah, that's me.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.900
I really feel bad, though, because your voice is pretty good.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.100
No, no, no, no. You got a great voice.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.900
So it's nice to have like a very deep guy and like, you know, hey, it's me.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.660
You guys waiting for a podcast?

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.640
So we're talking Donkey Kong today.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.380
Talking Donkey Kong. But most importantly, too, welcome to 1986.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.400
Oh, yeah. It feels so different now.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.600
Yeah. Things are different, especially in Japan.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.000
By this time in 1986, The Legend of Zelda is out in Japan.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.200
And Super Mario Brothers 2 is being released this same month as Donkey Kong is for NES.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.600
Even weirder, Donkey Kong was the launch title for the Japanese version.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.620
So that was like that.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.480
Donkey Kong, Donkey Kong Jr.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.520
And Popeye. Okay.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.400
That sounds really random.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.020
If you don't know, Miyamoto loved Popeye.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.720
And actually, Donkey Kong was going to be Popeye first.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.100
Wow. That was like a thing.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.980
Like, if you actually think about it, right?

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:38.200
If Mario was Popeye, olive oil was Pauline, and Bluto would have been the guy.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.520
Yeah, the big guy with the guns.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.620
The big Donkey Kong. He would have been Donkey Kong.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.440
So it makes sense. But they didn't get the rights in time to make that.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.500
So they were like, oh, I'll just make something funny instead.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.740
So he made Donkey Kong.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.680
And this is a funny story, because Donkey Kong actually doesn't translate well the way that Miyamoto thought it did.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.800
When they sent it over to Nintendo of America for testing, they were like, okay, but why Donkey Kong?

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.860
And he was like, well, you know, Donkey means stupid.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.680
So it's like he's a stupid ape.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:08.900
Oh, is that what that means?

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.600
Yeah, and they were like, well, no, Donkey doesn't mean that here.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.040
Like, that donkey's an animal.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.060
Like, you know, it's like they were associating it with, you know, Jack, blah.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.980
But we can say jackass.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:19.960
You can say jackass.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.360
This is jackass.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.940
We gave people enough warning, too.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.020
And the mom had time to turn off the podcast when I said jackass.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.380
So that's Donkey Kong in a nutshell.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:36.920
Also weird for 1986 is this is when the now you're playing with power.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:38.540
Oh, okay.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.240
That's when this catch line kind of like kicks off.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.560
Catchphrase, tagline. Catchphrase, tagline all together at the same time.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:52.020
It really kicks off here because now they're doing commercials because it's no longer just restricted to New York and L.A.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.540
It is now all throughout the country.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.480
You can finally get your Nintendos.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.060
So to most people, this would almost seem like it was also a launch title.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:01.640
Yeah, I think so.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:02.940
Like, yeah.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.620
In a weird way, though, too.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:10.040
Like, I think by this point, enough buzz has been generated by the New York and L.A.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.400
launches that like Electronic Games, which was one of the big magazines at the time.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.540
It's not the same as Electronic Gaming Monthly.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.100
Yeah. But Electronic Games was one of the big ones at the time.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:26.140
Did like this whole cover story on like Nintendo NESes are finally coming like around the States.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.260
And they were like, you know, you've been bugging us about it long enough.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.920
Like, so clearly like kids around were definitely interested in NES already just by what they were seeing.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.000
And I think part of the draw is games like Donkey Kong, which were huge hits in the arcade.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:48.700
But then also like seeing Super Mario Brothers and hearing about it and all that buzz was probably like another reason to want to own the console.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.140
Totally. So can we do first impressions?

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.340
Like how I don't know how new you are to Donkey Kong.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:04:00.820
Well, I mean, like, well, I guess first we should sort of describe Donkey Kong to whoever.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.540
It might help to do that.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:03.660
Yeah. Would you like to give that a stab?

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.400
I guess I could give that a shot.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:20.920
So in Donkey Kong, you play Mario as he tries and saves Pauline from the titular Donkey Kong who has run into a construction site.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.620
Yeah. Mario is like essentially in this game a carpenter.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.540
He's not a plumber. He's a carpenter.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.760
Yeah. Okay. Is there any wood?

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.840
No, but they described him as a carpenter in this game.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.160
Then he's also like doing the, you know, like in Wrecking Crew.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.820
Yeah. He's doing all that stuff too.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.220
Yeah. So an all around construction worker guy.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.060
Yeah. I think plumber only came into the equation with Mario Brothers.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.400
With the pipes and all that.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:53.660
So, yeah, you have to go up this incomplete tower to save Pauline from the giant ape.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:58.360
And you have in this version only three levels, which are all different.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:05.040
Very strange. Yeah. But it's a quick, it shows a quick story.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:13.300
Yeah. I think this is actually, I don't know if this is bold to say, but this might be like one of the first examples of a game with a story.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:19.680
Really? I mean, Pac-Man kind of has like that, they meet and everything and like those little cutscenes.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.180
But this is very complete.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.960
Yeah. I think prior to this, probably you could say that every game had justifications.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.980
Yeah. Like, I guess I understand the plot of asteroids.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.820
Yeah. You know, like you're in this space shuttle trying to shoot off asteroids before they hit Earth.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.900
But I mean, like Kung Fu, well, you're right.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:37.920
Kung Fu is not as.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:39.860
Yeah. Donkey Kong is.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:46.080
But no, that's a good point to bring up is that like if you're doing this chronologically, it is weird because it's like this game seems really primitive.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.040
Yeah. The only thing you can do in this game is jump.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.600
Yeah. And jumps from certain heights will kill you.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.040
Yeah. That was kind of annoying.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.380
But yeah, this.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:56.920
Yeah. So this actually was my first impression.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.600
If we're just going to jump into first impressions.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:06.900
First impressions is cool. I mean, I had thrown some quarters into the arcade cabinet before and immediately pretty much got run over by barrels.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:13.580
For some reason, I had very bad reflexes when I played this game in the past.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.660
So this is the first time I've ever actually played through it.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:20.880
On the NES hardware.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.800
Because I just, you know, I think it's worth throwing out there at this point.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:26.680
They are different.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:37.000
Yeah. I would actually argue, too, that there would be a disadvantage to being an NES player thinking you're good at Donkey Kong and then playing the arcade version.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.580
Yeah. So you're saying you had the arcade version experience first.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.180
I had the arcade version experience first, but I never got past the first stage.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.140
Okay. Yeah. And that would make some sense.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.920
Yeah. So everything after that was completely new to me.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:55.360
I mean, I've seen footage of, I mean, I've seen the documentary King of Kong.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.280
King of Kong. So I know a lot about.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:58.320
We'll have to talk about that later.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.820
Yeah. I know that there's a lot of lore behind that.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:11.860
So I've seen the other stages played out, but I had to learn it as I played, which I think a lot of people, when this game came out, were just like, oh, here's the levels that I know.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:14.960
And I thought it was fun.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:24.040
I thought that the jumping is, it's not Super Mario Brothers, but it's definitely also not Ice Climber.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:27.600
Yeah. And I think that's how we judge our jumping in games right now.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.800
It's like. On a scale from Ice Climber to Super Mario Brothers, where are you?

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:35.360
Yeah, exactly. Because it's like Kung Fu, you jump, but it's not necessary.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.140
You don't need to jump. You jump to dodge low knives, and that's about it.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:40.080
And snakes.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.720
The snakes, yeah. But if I could just jump in for a minute.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:51.720
Some things that I just wanted to touch off on my first impression is I can't really give a first impression here because I did play Donkey Kong a lot in the arcade.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:08:00.300
And playing this NES version, this is, I guess, my NES first impression, is it's a bit of a revelation because it's a little easier.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.720
The jump feels better.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.540
The jump, by all means, is definitely like over the barrels.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:11.140
I very rarely like clipped into one at just the edge and felt like I got screwed by that.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:18.020
The other thing is I think even the jump of like the we were talking about how like a jump from a far height will will kill you.

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:28.080
I think that like they were a little more lenient on that here, too, because in the arcade version, certain times I would jump just the smallest of gaps between two things.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:29.620
And they would register that as a kill.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:30.620
So I thought that was kind of cheap.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.500
But I'm definitely missing the inclusion of that third level.

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:37.060
Yeah. Because it goes in the arcade game.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:38.680
It's like how high can you climb?

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:42.160
And it's measured in Donkey Kongs, which are really strange.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:43.740
It's measured like 25 meters.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:45.240
I didn't know he was 25 meters tall.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:47.640
Yeah. So he's 25 meters tall.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.300
And so we're missing the 75 meter stage.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.000
It goes right to the 100 meter stage.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.120
But we'll talk about that when we go through the stages.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:09:02.180
One thing I just wanted to mention is Miyamoto, not exactly directly related to the port here of the NES version.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:03.800
So I don't know if he was actually a fan of this.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:07.580
Most of these, he was more concerned with like baseball.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:11.360
And because that was another one of like the Famicom early games.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.000
He was more concerned about making those games and getting them right for the system launch.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.160
That Donkey Kong by that point in his career that was like, oh, that was two years ago.

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:21.840
Like I'm kind of done with that.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.040
They had a separate team that was porting those games.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:28.500
Well, it's kind of like why would you work on the remaster or along those lines.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.280
Yeah, exactly. And so I don't know how he would feel about the game being a little easier.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.860
I don't think he ever intended to make it challenging.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.660
So do you think that the game is easier because of improvements to the system?

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.700
Improvements, yes. I think these are improvements.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:43.780
Okay. I do feel like.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:47.200
I don't think that you don't think that's like a dumbing down of the game, though.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:48.500
Yeah, I think that the.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:52.380
Yeah, I don't think. Well, I mean, obviously, if you remove a level kind of is that.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:56.980
But in terms of mechanics, I think Donkey Kong, the arcade game has that.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:06.320
We need quarters feel to it, you know, where similar to like if you've ever gone to the arcade recently and you play Pac-Man and it starts on speed 3000.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:08.680
Yeah. And you're like, I don't remember this.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:12.140
Like, the ghosts are only blue for half a second.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:14.160
It's like because they're eating your quarters.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.140
It doesn't have to be that way, but that's the way it is.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:22.400
Another fun thing that we didn't talk about that I just want to get out of the way before we go deeper into the game.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.220
Mario at this point is established and well known.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:30.260
But when this game came out in 1981, he was Jumpman.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:42.780
Jumpman. They only named him Mario when they when it went overseas to America because the landlord of the Nintendo of America building, his name was Mario and he looked like Mario.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:44.500
So they're like, oh, yeah, Mario.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.500
There you go. See, I didn't know that little bit of tidbit stories.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:54.760
Yeah, it's well, I'll have a little more tidbits later, but we we should probably get into the game and start with 25 millimeter.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.340
20, 25 meter.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:04.020
We should probably leave that in so everybody can know how silly I was saying that I was imagining, too.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.520
I even saw it on the screen like 25 mm, you know, like I don't know.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.180
Dungie Kong got really small.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:19.640
They removed all that stuff, though, from the game, too, which is weird because there's not even the awesome setup of him climbing up the ladders to the top of the building.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:26.820
There's none of that. All they do is they just they play that that very ominous theme over the title screen.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:40.860
And and I do like that, but also weird enough, they play the at the time, of course, it wasn't, but they play the now Donkey Kong Country theme song as well.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:42.660
The do do do.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.460
Oh, yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.900
Obviously, like at the time, they weren't thinking of that.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:02.600
No, but there it is.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.880
There it is. Donkey Kong Country theme song starts there.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:06.400
Not in Donkey Kong Country.

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:09.020
Yeah, it's very it's, you know, just a little sidebar.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:12.740
It's very strange to see to see these games.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:25.000
Well, right now it's only Donkey Kong, but like as somebody that grew up on Donkey Kong Country to see the the origins of this character and how unestablished he was in like any of his traits.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:30.800
And I would just like to offer the reverse opinion of a guy who didn't grow up with Donkey Kong Country.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:32.820
The CGI graphics did not.

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:34.640
They didn't do it for you.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:43.360
They didn't do it for me. So I ignored those games and I only know knew of Donkey Kong for the longest time as this version, you know, like this is how I thought of Donkey Kong.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:48.660
And it's something I do want to talk about after we play all the Donkey Kong games, one, junior three.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:50.720
And we have to talk about math.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:52.540
We will talk about the junior math.

00:12:52.580 --> 00:13:00.740
But I want to talk about is like Donkey Kong almost fades into obscurity until the Donkey Kong Country games like they're what revive him, essentially.

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:06.560
But Donkey Kong at this point is Nintendo's first character because Mario's just jump in.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:08.540
They didn't have big plans for him.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:16.480
We talked about in the Super Mario Brothers episode that that game wasn't even supposed to have Mario until they were just like, oh, the sales of Mario Brothers is doing well.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:22.500
You know, it's like it's really funny how things work out for Nintendo early on for things that they didn't intend.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:26.160
Yeah. So let's talk about 25 Meter.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.620
The very first course, it's very simple.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:32.060
And I like that, you know, the the zigzag approach to the stage.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:35.220
Everybody who's anybody who's played video games knows this level.

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:37.940
Yeah. I can't imagine not having seen it.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:39.100
You know what we're talking about.

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:41.940
There's a little barrel that's soon going to be lit on fire.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.000
And why do you think that barrel is on fire?

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.340
I don't know.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:53.060
You know, it's it's weird because I never really noticed that barrel until I was playing because it just seemed like, oh, that's the start point.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.980
Yeah. And it's like to enforce that you have no escape.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:57.140
Yeah. Must go up.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:02.060
Yeah, exactly. Like you're going to go up like in some in some way.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:08.820
If you can figure out a way to get over the barrel before it gets set on fire by the blue barrel that gets thrown down.

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:09.780
If you can figure it out.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.080
Maybe that's an alternate ending where Mario just leaves.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:14.340
And he's like, peace out.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:15.400
It's like Pauline.

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:17.600
Yeah, I could. I can do better than Pauline.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:25.920
But, you know, that's the 25 meter level essentially is just like a good way to figure out the jumping controls.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.920
It's a pretty straightforward design.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:31.620
I mean, it can get difficult.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.140
Especially after you've beaten the game a couple of times.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:35.480
Yeah. The difficulty keeps going up.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.020
And eventually you'll. You start throwing in different arcs.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:39.880
Yeah. There's that zigzag.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:42.720
Yeah. Which is only in the front half of the level though.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:43.620
So it's not too cheap.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:48.000
It would be really cheap if that barrel just zigzagged across the entire screen.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:53.360
Exactly. But the barrels, you know, for the most part they're pretty fair.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:59.740
I did find though sometimes that you have to have a really good jump when there's two barrels directly next to each other.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:03.980
Because it could be doubling the thing you have to clear.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:11.300
Right. And so if you're not kind of like in a perfect jump running scenario, you're not really going to be able to clear that one.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.260
And so that was the only. That's my only gripe.

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:14.740
Yeah. With the 25 meter level.

